WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.907
A payment service keeps a registry that maps a class to a service.

2
00:00:06.907 --> 00:00:10.159
PaymentGateway gets a slot, and so does FraudCheck.

3
00:00:10.367 --> 00:00:12.935
Now register a Repository&lt;Payment&gt;.

4
00:00:12.935 --> 00:00:16.972
Its key is its class, and the class is just Repository.

5
00:00:17.200 --> 00:00:19.703
Then register a Repository&lt;Refund&gt;.

6
00:00:19.703 --> 00:00:21.992
What does the registry hold now?

7
00:00:23.867 --> 00:00:24.916
One repository.

8
00:00:24.916 --> 00:00:31.069
The second key is the same Repository, so it replaced the first, and nothing complained.

9
00:00:31.967 --> 00:00:35.698
The key lost Payment because a class is not a type.

10
00:00:35.698 --> 00:00:42.796
One class, ArrayList, backs many types: ArrayList&lt;String&gt;, ArrayList&lt;String&gt;? and ArrayList&lt;Int&gt;.

11
00:00:42.967 --> 00:00:44.735
The types live in your code.

12
00:00:44.735 --> 00:00:48.335
The compiler checks them, then erases the type arguments.

13
00:00:48.533 --> 00:00:51.960
So at run time, an object carries only its class.

14
00:00:51.960 --> 00:00:57.485
An ArrayList&lt;String&gt; and an ArrayList&lt;Int&gt; get the same stamp: class ArrayList.

15
00:00:57.667 --> 00:01:02.239
Ask either one with x::class, and you get the same answer.

16
00:01:02.467 --> 00:01:06.680
That answer is a KClass, Kotlin's object for a class.

17
00:01:06.680 --> 00:01:09.541
Java's is a Class, from x.javaClass.

18
00:01:09.733 --> 00:01:11.986
They're two views of one class.

19
00:01:11.986 --> 00:01:17.943
On the JVM every KClass wraps a Class, and .java and .kotlin convert between them.

20
00:01:17.943 --> 00:01:22.084
Neither has type arguments, and neither is ever nullable.

21
00:01:22.267 --> 00:01:24.880
The full type is a third object, a KType.

22
00:01:24.880 --> 00:01:29.533
It holds the class, the type arguments, and whether the type is nullable.

23
00:01:29.733 --> 00:01:35.031
These three KTypes are all different, but their classifier is the same KClass.

24
00:01:35.233 --> 00:01:37.401
And no object can hand you a KType.

25
00:01:37.401 --> 00:01:43.844
The compiler writes one down where it still knows the type, at a call site: typeOf&lt;ArrayList&lt;String&gt;&gt;().

26
00:01:44.933 --> 00:01:49.783
So key the registry by KType, and the two repositories get two slots.

27
00:01:50.967 --> 00:01:53.086
Java has Class, but no KType.

28
00:01:53.086 --> 00:02:00.759
Its closest answer is a super type token: an anonymous subclass whose declared superclass keeps the type.

29
00:02:00.759 --> 00:02:02.293
Kotlin has all three.

30
00:02:03.667 --> 00:02:06.432
The rule: an object knows only its class.

31
00:02:06.432 --> 00:02:09.466
KClass and Class are two views of that class.

32
00:02:09.466 --> 00:02:14.524
Only a KType keeps the type arguments, and only the compiler can write one.

33
00:02:14.700 --> 00:02:15.569
Quick check.

34
00:02:15.569 --> 00:02:18.610
Do String and String? have the same class?

35
00:02:18.610 --> 00:02:20.565
Do they have the same type?

36
00:02:22.533 --> 00:02:24.572
Same class: String::class.

37
00:02:24.572 --> 00:02:28.648
Different types, because a KType counts nullability.
